<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Keller returns for her subsequent annual wellness visit, her first at this clinic; her initial wellness visit was with her previous physician. The visit was conducted through a qualified tactile interpreter, with the room arranged so her hand could rest on the interpreter&apos;s and Dr. Strand seated within reach, so she could read speech by touch when she wished. Her August cystitis cleared within three days on nitrofurantoin. She has had no falls since the one that brought her here in March, and five weeks ago her chair stand and balance had improved to the age norms. She does her strength and balance exercises daily. She has made two speaking trips this year with a sighted guide. She has no new concerns. She wants her advance care plan updated with her communication passport. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Subsequent annual wellness visit</td></tr><tr><td>Communication</td><td>Qualified tactile interpreter; double-length visit; materials in braille</td></tr><tr><td>Safety events</td><td>No falls since March; no emergency visits or hospital stays</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>No regular medicines; nitrofurantoin course in August completed</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Lifelong deafblindness; artificial eye; low bone mass without treatment indication; fall risk now reduced</td></tr><tr><td>Functional status</td><td>Independent in all activities of daily living; her assistant helps with driving, shopping, and print mail</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed at home on her braille display before the visit and reviewed with her through a qualified tactile interpreter. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Very good</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Lives with her long-time assistant and a dog; daily correspondence with friends and colleagues; regular visits from members of the deafblind community; no loneliness</td></tr><tr><td>Behavioral</td><td>Never smoker; a glass of wine with dinner a few times a week; walks outdoors daily with a sighted guide; exercises daily; sleeps seven hours</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent in bathing, dressing, eating, toileting, and transfers</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental activities of daily living</td><td>Manages her own money, medicines, and correspondence in braille; her assistant drives, shops, and reads print mail</td></tr><tr><td>Home safety</td><td>Handrails on both sides of every step, tactile edge strips, vibrating smoke and carbon monoxide alarms with a bed shaker, and a wearable alert button</td></tr><tr><td>Communication and sensory</td><td>Deafblind since infancy; uses tactile fingerspelling, speech, and braille; no change in the feeling in her hands</td></tr><tr><td>Bladder</td><td>No leakage; cystitis in August resolved</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>No concerns from her or her assistant</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

